  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nTONIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY NIGHT...The main concern is the \npotential for heavy rain and flooding across southern Minnesota.\nLatest guidance supports the greatest risk for thunderstorms \nFriday evening along and near I-90. These storms will have \nabundant moisture, and could be slow moving and/or back \nbuilding. Hourly rainfall rates of 2 to 3 inches would be \npossible, so any areas that see a couple hours of storms have a \npotential for rainfall totals of 4, 5, 6 inches, or more. No \nflood watches have been issued, but these would be needed later \nif the trends continue to highlight southeast Minnesota.\nGenerally in the area of Owatonna and Albert Lea and points \neast.\n\nElsewhere, widespread, soaking rain is still expected, but the \nrainfall rates will be much less given the lack of instability.\nForecast soundings off the RRFS and HRRR for MSP only have \nabout 0.25 inches in any given hour, so even though total \namounts of 1 to 2 inches of rain seems likely by the time the \nrain ends Friday night, the risk for flooding across central MN \nand west central MN is pretty low, especially considering the \ndry antecedent conditions.\n\nThe set up for all of this ties back to a shortwave that will lift \nup from the southwest later tonight. A stationary boundary that was \nlaid out west-to-east along the I-80 corridor will lift northward as \na warm front. Precipitable water values are in excess of 2 inches \nalong this warm front, and strong southeast winds along the front \nwill lead to widespread rain and storms in the Dakotas overnight. \nThese will move eastward into Minnesota and Wisconsin on Friday, but \nweaken as they run out of instability. This will lead to lower \nrainfall rates, generally around 0.25 inches per hour. The lone \nexception could be an enhancement of FGEN/rain along an elevated \nfront, but even then it would be tough to get rates over an inch per \nhour given the lack of instability.\n\nFarther south however is a different story. Forecast soundings show \nelevated instability building near I-90, and some HiRes \nguidance show thunderstorms developing Friday evening. This is \nthe main area of concern for flooding, generally after 6pm, and\nending by midnight.\n\nSATURDAY, SUNDAY AND NEXT WEEK...Rain will exit the region Friday \nnight as the warm front pushes south, leaving low clouds behind. A \nfew showers could redevelop on Saturday, main to the south along I-\n90 again. Given the clouds, northeast winds, and cooler airmass, \nafternoon highs will be in the mid 60s, giving a fall-like feel to \nthe weekend.\n\nLooking ahead, the aforementioned warm front will stall out across \nIowa and Illinois, keeping the rain chances to the south. There are \na few small chances for rain (10 to 20 percent) at times, but \noverall drier ensemble guidance shows drier weather for next\nweek.",
